**Analysis:**  
The waveform shows a sequential circuit where outputs p and q update on clock edges when input a is high, holding their values otherwise. This suggests a simple register with conditional updates. The pattern indicates p and q represent a counter or state register that increments or resets based on clock and input a.

**Implementation:**  
Using a sequential always block triggered by the clock edge, I'll implement non-blocking assignments for p and q, updating them only when a is high. This ensures proper sequential behavior matching the waveform's observed pattern.

[BEGIN]
module TopModule(
  input  clock,
  input  a,
  output reg p,
  output reg q
);

  always @(posedge clock) begin
    if (a) begin
      p <= p + 1;
      q <= q + 1;
    end
  end
endmodule
